<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     TIME DIFFERENCE SECTION & SIDE-BY-SIDE GRAPHIC
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section" id="time-difference" aria-labelledby="time-diff-heading">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Time Difference Mechanics</p>
    <h2 id="time-diff-heading">Analyzing the Time Gap: AEST vs EST</h2>
    <p>
      The time difference between Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST) and US Eastern Standard Time (EST) is exactly <strong>15 hours</strong>. Since AEST operates at UTC+10 and EST is fixed at UTC-5, eastern Australia runs fifteen hours ahead of Eastern Standard Time. When it is 8:00 AM EST in New York during winter, local clocks in Sydney and Melbourne show 11:00 PM AEST on the same calendar day.
    </p>
    <p>
      This time difference shifts when daylight saving rules apply. The United Kingdom and New Zealand observe daylight saving time on opposite cycles because they are located in different hemispheres. When Australia switches to AEDT (UTC+11) and the US is on EST (UTC-5), the gap increases to 16 hours. Conversely, when Australia returns to AEST (UTC+10) and the US shifts to Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4), the gap narrows to 14 hours.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Custom AEST vs EST Comparison Infographic (SVG) -->
