     COMMON MISTAKES & CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Confusions and Pitfalls in AEST to EST Calculations</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>Confusing AEST with AEDT</h3>
    <p>
      AEST is the winter standard time (UTC+10), while AEDT is the summer daylight saving time offset (UTC+11). Using the wrong offset when calculating time differences can result in a 1-hour scheduling error. Always verify the calendar date before applying the offset.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Eastern Time vs Eastern Standard Time</h3>
    <p>
      "Eastern Time" is a general term that refers to both EST (winter, UTC-5) and EDT (summer, UTC-4). If you assume Eastern Time is always EST, your meetings during summer months will be misaligned. Use our DST-aware calculator to determine the correct offset.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

